--- !u!114 &426185859
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 426185854}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "The Story of the Moth \n\nOne night, the whole family had gone to bed
    early\u2014except for my mother and me. I don\u2019t know why, but the two of
    us stayed up, sitting quietly together. \n\nThe candles were already put out
    using a curved tin blower. The room was dim, lit only by a coconut oil lamp.\n\nThat
    night, my mother was teaching me how to read a book called El Amigo de los Ni\xF1os.
    She grew impatient because I was reading poorly. She also scolded me for drawing
    silly pictures in the book. \n\nThen she said, \u201CJust listen,\u201D and began
    to read aloud herself. \n\nI got tired of listening to words I didn\u2019t understand.
    I turned my eyes to the little flame instead. It danced cheerfully. Some small
    moths were flying around it in circles. \n\nI happened to yawn. My mother saw
    that I was losing interest, so she stopped reading. \n\n\u201CI\u2019ll read
    you a very nice story now,\u201D she said. \n\n\u201CYou must listen.\u201D \n\nI
    opened my eyes wide. This sounded new and exciting. She began reading a fable
    about an old moth and a young moth, translating it into Tagalog as she read.\n\nFrom
    the very first sentence, I was interested. I stared at the lamp and watched the
    moths flying around it.\n\nThe story was like this: \n\nA mother moth once warned
    her daughter not to go near a flame. The light may look beautiful and inviting,
    but it was dangerous. It could burn anyone who got too close. \n\nThe mother
    moth said she herself had once flown too near the flame and barely escaped. She
    had half-burned her wings. \n\nThe young moth promised to obey. But soon, she
    began to wonder, \n\n\u201CWhy is my mother trying to scare me? Why should I
    close my eyes to such pretty light?\u201D \n\n\u201COld people are always afraid
    of everything! What harm can it do me if I go near\u2014just carefully? I\u2019ll
    have such a good story to tell if I look closer.\u201D\n\nSo she flew around
    the flame. At first, it felt pleasantly warm. That gave her more courage, so
    she flew closer and closer. \n\nBut then she got too near. Dazzled by the light,
    she fell into the flame and died.\n\nAs my mother tucked me into bed, she said:\n\n\u201CDon\u2019t
    be like the young moth. Don\u2019t disobey, or you may get hurt the same way.\u201D\n\nI
    don\u2019t remember if I answered her. But the story opened my eyes.\n\nI never
    saw moths the same way again. They were no longer just small insects. Now I believed
    they could talk. They could warn and give advice\u2014just like my mother.\n\nThe
    flame also looked different to me. It seemed more beautiful. It glowed brighter
    and felt more magical. I finally understood why moths flew around the light.\n\n\u2013
    THE END \u2013"
